<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Antalcidas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">387–386 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as King's Peace · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of Antalcidas, or King's Peace, was the settlement imposed by the Persian king Artaxerxes II in 387–386 BCE, negotiated by the Spartan Antalcidas, that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a>. It recognised Persian rule over the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and declared all other Greek cities autonomous. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> used the autonomy clause to break up rival leagues such as the Boeotian League.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-antalcidas.jpg" alt="Peace of Antalcidas" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carleton L. (Carleton Lewis), (1866-1948) Publication date 1918-1922 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King%27s_Peace_387_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
